{# THE REGISTER — every area this installation manages, drawn as a wall of
   workspaces: one card per area, its live operational pulse on the face and its
   sub-app one click behind. It reads the same at four cards or forty.

   Design: areas/index.html in the design workspace (the graduated card gallery,
   source presets/areas-index/a.html).

   THE AREA PAGE OWNS THE FRAME, A MODULE OWNS THE CONTENT. A card's face, its
   identity line, its "modules live" count and its "last check-in" are the
   the area page's; every operational figure — the stat cells, the "out right now" chip,
   the alert flag — arrived through the overview contributions and is laid out without the
   the area page understanding it. That is what lets a card read honestly when a module
   leaves: its figures stop arriving and no hard-coded cell is left behind. See
   src/Service/AreaRegister.php.

   THE CARD FACE is the area's real boundary, framed to its own bounding box and
   drawn as an outline over a neutral imagery-toned ground. The graduated design
   asks for that outline over a STATIC SATELLITE snippet, cached per area; the
   satellite raster half is deferred (the atlas exposes no server-side static
   render yet) — see src/Service/AreaThumbnailer.php. A boundary-less area shows
   the neutral ground alone.

   THE PLATE CARRIES NO WIDGET INDEX. The design file prints one in the tab
   (`PL·31`); that is the workspace's referencing system and not product. See
   tests/Unit/Template/NoWorkshopLabelsTest.php. #}
{% extends '@Shell/page.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Areas{% endblock %}

{% block stylesheets %}
    {{ parent() }}
    {{ include('@Area/_stylesheets.html.twig') }}
{% endblock %}

{# The register is not inside an area, so it fills the tab socket with nothing
   rather than inheriting the area strip. #}
{% block shell_page_tabs %}{% endblock %}

{% block shell_breadcrumbs %}<a href="{{ path('area_index') }}">uhifadhi</a> / areas{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_title %}Areas{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_subtitle %}The org&rsquo;s register of protected areas, drawn as a wall of workspaces. Each card is an area you manage &mdash; its live operational pulse on the face, its sub-app one click behind.{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page_actions %}
    {# THE WIDGET-LIBRARY ADOPT-CONTROL — the same control, in the same place, as
       every widget surface. Its library (the five preset directions this register
       was drawn in) is a follow-up slice; the button is wired to where that route
       will live. A literal href rather than path(), on purpose: the route is not
       mounted yet, so generating it would fail — this is the same "the door is
       named before it is built" is the reassurance the New area button carries. #}
    <a class="tgl w-act" href="/areas/widgets">{{ ux_icon('shell:layout-grid') }} Widget library</a>
    {# THE BUTTON CARRIES THE PERMISSION, not just the screen behind it: a control
       that opens onto a refusal teaches somebody the product is broken. #}
    {% if is_granted('area.create') %}
        <a class="cta" href="{{ path('area_new') }}">{{ ux_icon('shell:plus') }} New area</a>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{% block shell_page %}
    {% if rows is empty %}
        {# AN INSTALLATION WITH NO AREAS IS NEW, NOT BROKEN. An empty wall reads
           as a page that failed to load; this says what is true and what to do. #}
        <div class="c">
            <p class="zeb">No areas yet. An area is the named piece of ground this
                installation manages &mdash; the axis everything else is filed under,
                so nothing else has anywhere to go until one exists.</p>
            <p class="zeb"><span class="mono d" style="font-size:10.5px">Areas arrive from whatever seeds this installation, or from a boundary import.</span></p>
            {% if is_granted('area.create') %}
                <p class="zeb"><a class="cta" href="{{ path('area_new') }}">{{ ux_icon('shell:plus') }} New area</a></p>
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    {% else %}
        {# SEARCH, THE FILTER PILLS WITH THEIR LIVE COUNTS, AND THE SORT STATE —
           all three narrow the SAME rendered cards on the client, drawn by the
           shared wall partial the widget library's "Wall of workspaces" preview
           renders too, so the register landing and its preset can never drift.

           THE PILLS ARE ONLY THE ONES THAT CAN MOVE. Live and awaiting-setup are
           the registry's ledger; alerts is the attention contribution. All three change with
           the installation, so none is a filter that can only ever return
           everything or nothing. #}
        {{ include('@Area/area/_register_cards.html.twig') }}
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}
